-- title:
--   Invertibility of restricted section and divisor ideals on a curve model
-- statement:
--   Let $R$ be a commutative ring, $C$ a scheme and $c\colon C\to\operatorname{Spec}R$ a separated morphism, and let $U\subseteq C$ be an open subscheme such that the composite $U\hookrightarrow C\xrightarrow{c}\operatorname{Spec}R$ is smooth of relative dimension $1$. Let $\varepsilon$ be a section of $c$, i.e. a morphism $\operatorname{Spec}R\to C$ with $\varepsilon\circ c$ the identity, whose image lies in $U$. Let $t\colon T\to\operatorname{Spec}R$ be a morphism, $r,e$ natural numbers, and $D$ a relative effective Cartier divisor of degree $e$ for $c$ over $t$: an ideal sheaf datum $D.I$ on $C\times_{\operatorname{Spec}R}T$ whose closed subscheme is finite, flat and locally of finite presentation over $T$ with fibre rank $e$ at every point of $T$, and with $\operatorname{supp}D.I$ contained in the preimage of $U$ under the first projection. Let $k$ be a field and $\mathrm{pt}\colon\operatorname{Spec}k\to T$ a point, and write $X:=(C\times_{\operatorname{Spec}R}T)\times_T\operatorname{Spec}k$ for the fibre, with its projection $\mathrm{fibreAt}$, the second projection, to $\operatorname{Spec}k$. Let $F_1$ be a field extension of $k$ and $M_1$ a `CurveModel` for $F_1/k$: an integral scheme $M_1.C$, proper and smooth of relative dimension $1$ over $\operatorname{Spec}k$, whose function field is identified with $F_1$ compatibly with $k$, whose closed points parametrise bijectively the places of $F_1/k$ by way of their stalks, and in which every finite set of points lies in an affine open. Let $i_1\colon M_1.C\to X$ be a morphism over $\operatorname{Spec}k$ (that is, $i_1$ followed by $\mathrm{fibreAt}$ equals the structure morphism of $M_1$), and let $W_1\subseteq X$ be an open subscheme such that the inclusion of $i_1^{-1}W_1$ followed by $i_1$ is an open immersion, with $W_1$ contained in the image of $i_1$ on points, and such that every point of $X$ whose image under the first projection $X\to C\times_{\operatorname{Spec}R}T$ lies either in $\operatorname{supp}D.I$ or in the image of $\mathrm{rigSection}\,c\,t\,\varepsilon\colon T\to C\times_{\operatorname{Spec}R}T$ belongs to $W_1$. Then, writing $\psi$ for $i_1$ followed by the first projection $X\to C\times_{\operatorname{Spec}R}T$, both the inverse-image ideal sheaf datum $\psi^{*}\big((\operatorname{sectionIdeal}c\,\varepsilon\,t)^{r}\big)$, where $\operatorname{sectionIdeal}c\,\varepsilon\,t$ is the kernel ideal sheaf datum of $\mathrm{rigSection}\,c\,t\,\varepsilon$, and $\psi^{*}(D.I)$ are invertible on $M_1.C$, invertibility meaning that every point has an affine open $V$ and $f\in\Gamma(V)$ with the point in the basic open of $f$ and the ideal on the affine basic open of $f$ generated by a single non-zero-divisor.
--
--   This is the transport step which moves invertibility of the ideal sheaves of the $r$-fold section divisor $r\varepsilon$ and of the relative divisor $D$ from the total space $C\times_R T$ down to a curve model mapping into the fibre over a field-valued point: upstairs both ideals are invertible because the section and the support of $D$ lie in the locus where $c$ is smooth of relative dimension one, and downstairs invertibility is local and the relevant supports lie in the open where the map is an open immersion. It is used in the computation of the Euler characteristic of the pullback to the fibre of the tensor product of the fibre module with the section twist and the divisor ideal module.
